providers-pinned.remediation:
Pin each provider to an exact version, and commit the lock file `terraform init` writes:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "6.4.0"
    }
  }
}
```

```bash
terraform init
git add .terraform.lock.hcl
```

With `~> 6.0`, a runner can resolve a version nobody reviewed, so the apply can run against a provider the approved plan never saw. The lock file records checksums as well as versions, so a swapped provider shows up as a mismatch instead of looking like an ordinary upgrade.

Reusable modules are the exception and declare a range instead, because the root that calls them does the pinning. List your module directories in `modulePaths` and they are skipped.

state-locking.remediation:
Enable locking on the backend. Since Terraform 1.10, S3 can hold the lock itself:

```hcl
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket       = "company-terraform-state"
    key          = "live/main/terraform.tfstate"
    region       = "eu-west-1"
    use_lockfile = true
  }
}
```

Older configurations use a `dynamodb_table` instead, and both are recognised. Without a lock, two overlapping applies each write a state file that describes only their own changes, and the resources the other one created become unmanaged and invisible.

state-encrypted.remediation:
Enable encryption on the backend:

```hcl
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket     = "company-terraform-state"
    key        = "live/main/terraform.tfstate"
    region     = "eu-west-1"
    encrypt    = true
    kms_key_id = "arn:aws:kms:eu-west-1:111122223333:key/company-terraform"
  }
}
```

State contains every attribute of every resource, including the database password the provider generated and the private keys it holds. An unencrypted state file leaves all of that in object storage in the clear.
